<Base
  title="About"
  description="On the Rise is a small, honest publication on rising dev tools — what it is, who runs it, and why the method is shown."
  path="/about/"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      <strong>On the Rise</strong> is a small publication about developer tools that are gaining
      momentum — and about the decisions developers actually face when choosing between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      It exists because most "X vs Y" articles are written to rank, not to help. They copy each
      other's feature tables, hide the tradeoffs, and skip the part where a real developer has to
      make a real call. We try to be the opposite: fewer posts, clearer verdicts, evidence shown.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is deliberately transparent about its own construction: the topic method is
      published in full (<a href="/method/">see how we pick topics</a>), and the site's own launch
      — what worked, what ranked, what didn't — will be documented with real data as it happens.
      This is a working test project: a small site built in the open, where every technique and
      result is written down. If something here fails, the failure gets documented too.
    </p>
    <p>Built with <a href="https://astro.build" rel="noopener">Astro</a>. Hosted free. No tracking scripts.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
